# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 0 — the immutable source corpus.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `raw/` holds the original source records: every CBDB row, and later the
# original bytes of every web page and every book. Everything above it —
# entities, indexes, SQLite, the site — can be rebuilt from here. Nothing here
# can be rebuilt from above.
#
# That asymmetry makes `raw/` the ONLY layer that must be backed up. Losing it
# means losing the data; losing `data/` means re-running a transformer.
#
# 2,472,329 CBDB rows / 290 MB gzipped (was 1,774 MB as plain JSON, measured
# 12.4% compression on repeated JSON field names). Not committed: it is large
# and, unlike the site, cannot be regenerated from anything in the repo.
/raw/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authoritative corpus is NOT in version control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `data/` holds the full CBDB import — 486,788 persons, ~610 MB, and growing. It is
# the local working store that the admin UI, the CLI and every importer read and
# write. It is not a build artefact (it cannot be regenerated from anything in this
# repo), so losing it means losing the data, not rebuilding it.
#
# The published site is a *separate tree* (`site/`), generated from `data/` by
# `npm run build:site`, filtered to persons from 1900 CE onward. That is what git
# tracks and what gets deployed.
#
# Everything under data/ is therefore ignored. Keep backups outside the repo.
#
# NOTE: data/ is no longer the sole authority. The hash-chained corpus in raw/
# is now the layer that cannot be rebuilt, and data/ is derived from it. The
# lines below stay because the very large historical data/ tree must not enter
# git either.
#
# The leading slash is load-bearing: an unanchored `data/` also matches
# `site/data/`, which silently stripped the published tree's actual content from
# git — leaving a site that deployed with no entities at all. Always anchor.
/data/*
# 先排除内容、再逐个放行。git 对「目录已被排除」的路径无法用 `!` 救回：
# `/data/` 会把整个目录剪掉，后面的 `!/data/commits/` 就永远不生效。
# 必须写成 `/data/*` 才能让子项有被重新包含的机会。
!/data/commits/

# Built site data is the opposite: it is committed on purpose. Re-assert it so a
# future broad ignore pattern cannot cannibalise it again.
!/site/data/

# Commit log is the audit trail: append-only, one JSON per line.
#
# It must be in git. `commits` answers "who changed what, when, based on which
# raw record" — the question you ask after something looks wrong. A local-only
# log would be lost exactly when you need it (disk failure, bad rebuild).
#
# The `raw/` records it points at are NOT in git (they are large and
# reconstructible from the upstream sources), so the log names them by rawId;
# that indirection is intentional.
!/data/commits/

# Entity shards are the AUTHORITATIVE store, not a generated artifact.
#
# This block used to say "reproducible from canonical indexes, regenerate with
# npm run shard:data". That was wrong and it caused real data loss: index.json
# holds SUMMARY rows (id/name/tags/summary/detail), so any script that
# "regenerated" shards from it wrote summaries where entities belong, and 5269
# shards lost their props. shard_data.js was one-way (it overwrote index.json
# with the summary it had just derived) and so could not be re-run safely.
#
# The indexes are now generated FROM the shards by build_index.js. Shards never
# enter git; treat a missing shard as data loss to be restored from a backup,
# never as something to rebuild from index.json.
data/persons/entities/
data/organizations/entities/
data/events/entities/
data/things/entities/
data/evidence/entities/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The published site IS in version control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `site/` is generated, but it is the deployable artefact and it is small
# (~17 MB / ~7.7k files, of which ~3.8k are static entity pages). Committing it
# means GitHub Pages can serve it directly with no build step on the server.
# Rebuild it with `npm run build:site` after any data change.
#
# Nothing inside site/ should be edited by hand: fix the source in js/, css/,
# index.html or data/, then rebuild.

# Scratch output from one-off verification scripts
_cdp_check.js
_render_test.js
_app_render_test.js
